In this equations, (1) relies on the log sum inequality, while (2) assumes the following inequality

$$
\begin{array}{l} \mathrm {H} _ {\boldsymbol {\theta}} (X) = - \sum_ {x \in \mathcal {X}} p (x) \log p _ {\boldsymbol {\theta}} (x \mid \mathbf {d} _ {N}) (56a) \\ \leq - \sum_ {x \in \mathcal {X}} p (x) \log \sum_ {y \in \mathcal {Y}} p _ {\boldsymbol {\theta}} (x \mid y, \mathbf {d} _ {N}) p (y) (56b) \\ \end{array}
$$

This is equivalent to our assumption that this agent's estimate of $p_{\theta}(x \mid \mathbf{d}_N)$ is tighter than if the agent marginalised its beliefs over $y$ . While not necessarily true, in practice, if $X$ is discrete and has a small cardinality $|\mathcal{X}|$ , a simple Laplace smoothed estimate of $p_{\theta}(x \mid \mathbf{d}_N)$ is likely to result in this inequality. One could instead assume an agent which uses a Monte Carlo sampling approximation for estimating $p_{\theta}(x \mid \mathbf{d}_N)$ from $p_{\theta}(x \mid y, \mathbf{d}_N)$ . This would switch the inequality (2) for an approximation, and result in an expected lower bound instead

$$
\mathrm {I} _ {\boldsymbol {\theta}} (Y \rightarrow X \mid \mathbf {d} _ {N}) \lesssim \mathrm {I} (X; Y) \tag {57}
$$

# G Proof of the Convergence to Mutual Information, Theorem 4

Theorem 4 If we assume a Bayesian agent's set of beliefs and prior are well-formed and meet the conditions of Bernstein-von Mises Theorem (pg. 339, Bickel and Doksum, 2001). Then,

$$
\lim  _ {N \rightarrow \infty} \mathrm {I} _ {\boldsymbol {\theta}} (Y \rightarrow X \mid \mathbf {d} _ {N}) = \mathrm {I} (X; Y) \tag {58}
$$

